<!--
    Stack blocks: the invoke function named after its creator and typed, the
    captures typed, a `__block` cell typed, the creator's frame typed.

    Issue: a block built on a method's frame (`isa = _NSConcreteStackBlock`, the
    invoke and descriptor addresses stored after it) left its invoke function a
    `sub_` with an untyped first argument, so a captured variable read as an
    offset off an unknown pointer, and the creator read as a run of stores to
    numbered locals.

    Fix: `analyzers/objc/kuna_objcblocks.rs (stack_blocks)` scans each arm64
    function with a register state (`adrp`/`adr`/`add`/`sub`/`mov`/`movz`/`movk`,
    `ldr` immediate and literal, `str`/`stur`/`stp` of every width to `sp` or a
    frame address): the isa's GOT load stored to the frame, the invoke and
    descriptor 16 and 24 bytes after it, every capture after them, and a
    `__block` cell (`isa = 0, forwarding = &cell, flags|size, value`). The
    invoke is named `<creator>_block_invoke`, typed from the descriptor's
    signature, with `Block_layout_<invoke>` carrying one typed field per
    capture - the copy helper's `_Block_object_assign` flags (3 object, 7
    block, 8 byref) and `objc_retain` calls say which are objects, blocks and
    cells, the creator's store says the width of the rest - and
    `Block_byref_<size>_<width>` for a cell. The creator gets `block` and
    `cell_<k>` as typed locals.

    Pass 1 (`objc off`): `sub_100000930`. Pass 2 (`objc on`):
    `-[Runner tally:scale:]_block_invoke(Block_layout_100000930 *block,long arg1)`
    reading `block->capture_0` (self, objc_object *), `block->capture_1->forwarding->value`
    (the `__block long total`), `capture_2` (double s), `capture_3` (int step);
    the creator stores `block.block.invoke`, `block.capture_1 = &cell_1`,
    `cell_1.forwarding = block.capture_1`.
-->
